211 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 158 0 R
/E (A particularly innovative approach has been to exploit bacterial pathogens to fight cancer. A connection between the two was first made around 4,000 years ago, when local infection caused tumour eradication in some patients, though this was not taken advantage of until the 20th century \(Kwon et al., 2024\).  Progress since then has been swift, with many developments in engineering bacteria for immunotherapy. Some of these have been proven and adopted as widely accepted treatments. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
212 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 158 0 R
/E (A first-line treatment for high-risk superficial bladder cancer is intravesical BCG, a live form of Mycobacterium bovis \(closely related to human tuberculosis\) delivered to the bladder via catheter. It has significantly higher odds of a complete response than intravesical chemotherapy \(Sylvester et al., 2005\).)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
