235 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 161 0 R
/E (Early clinical studies provided initial evidence of the therapeutic potential of this approach. Sahin et al. demonstrated that personalised RNA vaccines elicited broad immune responses in patients with advanced melanoma. At the same time, Ott et al. reported that personalised neoantigen vaccination was safe, feasible, and capable of inducing durable T-cell immunity. These findings provided the foundation for the Phase IIb KEYNOTE-942 trial, in which the personalised mRNA vaccine mRNA-4157 \(V940\), administered alongside pembrolizumab, significantly reduced the risk of recurrence or death compared with pembrolizumab alone in patients with resected high-risk melanoma. This trial constitutes the strongest clinical evidence to date supporting personalised neoantigen vaccination.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
236 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 161 0 R
/E (Several challenges remain before personalised mRNA cancer vaccines can be adopted as routine clinical treatment. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
237 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 161 0 R
/E (A substantial proportion of tumour mutations fail to produce effective neoantigens, tumour cells can evade immune detection through loss of neoantigen expression or reduced antigen presentation, and manufacturing an individualised vaccine for each patient remains costly and time-intensive. Recent reviews indicate that continued progress in neoantigen identification, mRNA vaccine design, and clinical implementation is likely to extend this approach to a wider range of malignancies, consolidating its position as one of the most promising directions in precision cancer immunotherapy.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
