/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 163 0 R
/T (PD-1 is a receptor found on activated T-cells which binds to PD-L1 on other cells. This transmits an inhibitory signal, recuding cytokine production, proliferation, and survival, which prevents excessive immune activation. However, many tumours overexpress PD-L1, which allows them to send inhibitory signals that help them evade immune destruction.)
/E (PD-1 is a receptor found on activated T-cells which binds to PD-L1 on other cells. This transmits an inhibitory signal, recuding cytokine production, proliferation, and survival, which prevents excessive immune activation. However, many tumours overexpress PD-L1, which allows them to send inhibitory signals that help them evade immune destruction.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
251 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 163 0 R
/T (CTLA-4 is an inhibitory receptor found on activated T-cells. It competes with the stimulatory receptor CD28 to bind to B7 proteins on antigen-presenting cells. Normally, the interaction between CD28 and B7 triggers T-cell activation, proliferation, and survival pathways. However, as CTLA-4 binds B7 with a higher affinity, it can prevent the release of these costimulatory signals to act as an immune checkpoint.)
/E (CTLA-4 is an inhibitory receptor found on activated T-cells. It competes with the stimulatory receptor CD28 to bind to B7 proteins on antigen-presenting cells. Normally, the interaction between CD28 and B7 triggers T-cell activation, proliferation, and survival pathways. However, as CTLA-4 binds B7 with a higher affinity, it can prevent the release of these costimulatory signals to act as an immune checkpoint.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
