255 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 164 0 R
/T (The future of immune checkpoint inhibition is focused on combination therapies. Using ICIs with chemotherapy and radiotherapy has shown improved response rates. However, this comes alongside an increased incidence and severity of irAEs.)
/E (The future of immune checkpoint inhibition is focused on combination therapies. Using ICIs with chemotherapy and radiotherapy has shown improved response rates. However, this comes alongside an increased incidence and severity of irAEs.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
256 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 164 0 R
/T (Immune checkpoint inhibitors represent one of the most significant advances in modern cancer treatment. By harnessing the pathways of the immune system, these therapies have transformed the management of several cancers and reshaped our understanding of tumour biology and immunology. With further research, ICIs have the potential to form an increasingly accessible and effective treatment in the years to come.)
/E (Immune checkpoint inhibitors represent one of the most significant advances in modern cancer treatment. By harnessing the pathways of the immune system, these therapies have transformed the management of several cancers and reshaped our understanding of tumour biology and immunology. With further research, ICIs have the potential to form an increasingly accessible and effective treatment in the years to come.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
257 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 164 0 R
/T (Immune checkpoint inhibitors are monoclonal antibodies that inhibit the interaction between immune checkpoint molecules and T cells, allowing T cells to remain activated and attack cancer cells. In recent years, several drugs have been approved to target PD-1, PD-L1, and CTLA-4. Blocking PD-1 prevents inhibitory signalling within activated T-cells, whereas blocking PD-L1 prevents tumour cells from engaging PD-1, both acting to prolong the T-cell response. In contrast, inhibiting CTLA-4 restores CD28-mediated co-stimulatory signalling, promoting T-cell activation and expansion.)
/E (Immune checkpoint inhibitors are monoclonal antibodies that inhibit the interaction between immune checkpoint molecules and T cells, allowing T cells to remain activated and attack cancer cells. In recent years, several drugs have been approved to target PD-1, PD-L1, and CTLA-4. Blocking PD-1 prevents inhibitory signalling within activated T-cells, whereas blocking PD-L1 prevents tumour cells from engaging PD-1, both acting to prolong the T-cell response. In contrast, inhibiting CTLA-4 restores CD28-mediated co-stimulatory signalling, promoting T-cell activation and expansion.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
