/Title (In contrast to more traditional vaccines, which serve as preventative action against infectious diseases, personalised mRNA cancer vaccines are designed to elicit an immune response against cancer already present in the body.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 778 0 R
/Next 787 0 R
>>
endobj
786 0 obj
<<
/Title (CTLA-4 is an inhibitory receptor found on activated T-cells. It competes with the stimulatory receptor CD28 to bind to B7 proteins on antigen-presenting cells. Normally, the interaction between CD28 and B7 triggers T-cell activation, proliferation, and survival pathways. However, as CTLA-4 binds B7 with a higher affinity, it can prevent the release of these costimulatory signals to act as an immune checkpoint.)
/Dest [18 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 788 0 R
/Next 779 0 R
>>
endobj
787 0 obj
<<
/Title (The main targets of these vaccines are neoantigens, abnormal proteins resulting from mutations in the cancerous tumour cells. Because neoantigens are absent from healthy tissues, the immune system can readily recognise them as foreign and mount targeted responses to cells which express them. This separates neoantigens from other tumour-associated antigens, which are also found in normal tissues and are frequently subject to immune tolerance. As a result, neoantigens are better-suited targets for mRNA vaccines, allowing a stronger, more specific immune response while minimising damage to healthy cells.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 785 0 R
/Next 789 0 R
>>
endobj
788 0 obj
<<
/Title (PD-1 is a receptor found on activated T-cells which binds to PD-L1 on other cells. This transmits an inhibitory signal, recuding cytokine production, proliferation, and survival, which prevents excessive immune activation. However, many tumours overexpress PD-L1, which allows them to send inhibitory signals that help them evade immune destruction.)
/Dest [18 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 790 0 R
/Next 786 0 R
>>
endobj
789 0 obj
<<
/Title (Constructing a personalised vaccine begins with sequencing DNA and RNA from a patient's tumour and creating a profile of mutations unique to that individual's cancer.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 787 0 R
/Next 791 0 R
>>
endobj
790 0 obj
<<
/Title (Immune checkpoints are the result of interactions between cell receptors and ligands. Two of the best-characterised checkpoint pathways are programmed cell death protein 1 \(PD-1\) and cytotoxic T-lymphocyte-associated protein 4 \(CTLA-4\).)
/Dest [18 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 792 0 R
/Next 788 0 R
>>
endobj
